    ("gov_m2_34", "Nationalism: Early Newspapers", "2013", "The 'West African Pilot' newspaper, which served as a potent instrument of militant nationalist struggle in Nigeria, was established by:", ["Dr. Nnamdi Azikiwe", "Herbert Macaulay", "Obafemi Awolowo", "Ernest Ikoli"], 0, "Dr. Nnamdi Azikiwe launched the West African Pilot in 1937 with the motto 'Show the light and the people will find the way'.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m2_35", "Pre-Colonial Systems: Caliphate Emirate", "2012", "The supreme religious and political head of the Sokoto Caliphate was the:", ["Sultan of Sokoto (Amir al-Mu'minin)", "Shehu of Borno", "Emir of Kano", "Waziri"], 0, "The Sultan of Sokoto was the Commander of the Faithful (Amir al-Mu'minin) exercising ultimate spiritual and political leadership over all emirates.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m2_36", "Colonial Administration: Indirect Rule Taxes", "2014", "Under Indirect Rule in Northern Nigeria, the direct cattle tax collected from pastoralists was known as:", ["Jangali", "Kharaj", "Zakat", "Kurdin Kasa"], 0, "Jangali was the annual tax levied on cattle and livestock in the Northern emirates.", "Easy"),
    ("gov_m2_37", "Colonial Administration: Aba Riots 1929", "2011", "The primary catalyst for the 1929 Aba Women's Riots in Eastern Nigeria was the rumour regarding:", ["Taxation of women following head counts by Warrant Chiefs", "The abolition of palm oil farming", "The introduction of school fees", "The ban on female voting"], 0, "The census conducted by Warrant Chief Okugo in Oloko triggered widespread protests against perceived plans to tax women.", "Easy"),
